WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.944
Two identical GPUs simplify driver features, memory balance, expected speed, replacement, and split planning.

00:00:06.944 --> 00:00:13.440
They do not eliminate communication overhead, slot, lane, power, cooling, or runtime requirements.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:22.624
Mixed NVIDIA GPUs may be practical when reusing hardware, but different memory sizes and compute capabilities complicate balanced splits.

00:00:22.624 --> 00:00:30.464
The faster card may wait for the slower one, while the smaller card limits some strategies.

00:00:30.464 --> 00:00:38.304
A GPU plus CPU fallback can fit a model economically when interactive speed is not critical.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:46.144
It should be benchmarked with realistic context because CPU-resident work and memory transfer may dominate generation.

00:00:46.144 --> 00:00:53.088
A workstation tower centralizes storage and management but concentrates heat, noise, power, and failure.

00:00:53.088 --> 00:01:01.824
Used datacenter GPUs can offer memory value yet may require specialized cooling, power, drivers, brackets, or display arrangements.

00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:08.320
External GPU experiments are useful for learning but add enclosure and interface constraints.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:15.264
A production design should not assume an experimental link offers desktop-slot behavior or support.

00:01:15.264 --> 00:01:20.416
Multiple single-GPU servers can isolate models, users, failures, and upgrades.

00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:25.120
Networking and orchestration remain, but each node is simpler.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:32.961
Separate machines are often better when the goal is parallel workloads rather than one split model.

00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:40.353
A scheduler should understand model residency, memory headroom, queue length, request type, and device health.

00:01:40.353 --> 00:01:47.297
Round-robin routing alone can overload a smaller GPU or repeatedly trigger expensive model swaps.
